  "answer": "CDK5-dependent NUP98 Ser606 phosphorylation initiates transport failure in TARDBP-mutant motor neurons\n\nHypothesis. In TARDBP-mutant ALS motor neurons, increased CDK5-dependent phosphorylation of the candidate site NUP98 Ser606 reduces NUP98 retention at nuclear pores, impairing active nuclear import before persistent cytoplasmic TDP-43 assemblies develop. Reduced import then promotes nuclear TDP-43 depletion, cytoplasmic assembly formation, and neuronal dysfunction; subsequent assemblies amplify transport failure. Here, “initiating” means upstream of the transport defect and persistent assemblies, not necessarily the earliest consequence of the mutation. This is a proposed mechanism in a defined genetic context, not a universal explanation of ALS.\n\nSupported premises. Disease-linked TDP-43 can disrupt nucleoporin localization and nuclear transport, and related pore pathology occurs in ALS tissue [S1]. Persistent cytoplasmic TDP-43 assemblies can impair import, deplete nuclear TDP-43, and accompany cell death without conventional stress granules [S3]. Conversely, cellular stress can sequester transport factors into stress granules and impair transport [S4]. These findings support a transport–TDP-43 relationship but do not establish nucleoporin phosphorylation as its starting point. C9orf72-associated transport dysfunction provides a distinct genetic-context-specific route and should not be assumed to share the proposed initiating lesion [S2].\n\nSpeculative bridges. Selection of NUP98 as a nucleoporin rests on unverified prior knowledge outside this packet. Ser606 is a candidate site whose identity and numbering must first be confirmed in the expressed isoform. CDK5 phosphorylation of this site, phosphorylation-dependent loss of pore retention, and the resulting import defect are explicit assumptions, unsupported by [S1]–[S4]. The further claim that import failure precedes and promotes persistent TDP-43 assemblies is also speculative: the supplied studies support the reverse direction more directly. Phosphorylated TDP-43 in [S3] is not evidence for phosphorylated NUP98.\n\nDiscriminating preclinical strategy. Use patient-derived TARDBP-mutant spinal motor neurons and isogenic mutation-corrected controls, with independent clones and differentiation batches. Combine longitudinal imaging with matched sampling for phosphosite-resolved mass spectrometry. Measure Ser606 occupancy normalized to total NUP98, pore-associated NUP98, import-reporter kinetics, nuclear TDP-43, persistent cytoplasmic assemblies, stress granules, transport-factor localization, axonal integrity, and survival. The predicted sequence is phosphorylation, NUP98 displacement, import impairment, then persistent assemblies and dysfunction; timing alone would not establish causation.\n\nTest necessity using endogenous NUP98-S606A editing, synonymous-edit controls, and reversion to wild type. Require comparable baseline NUP98 abundance, pore localization, import, and neuronal maturation. Independently suppress CDK5 activity and measure target-site engagement; kinase inhibition without reduced Ser606 occupancy would not test the proposed link. Protection by both interventions, with little additional protection when combined, would support a shared pathway, although ceiling effects must be excluded.\n\nFor supportive sufficiency evidence, transiently recruit active CDK5 to NUP98 in corrected neurons, comparing wild-type and S606A backgrounds with catalytically inactive recruitment controls. Require verified site phosphorylation and an import defect before detectable assemblies or generalized injury. Because recruitment could modify other substrates, it cannot alone prove site-specific sufficiency. Phosphomimetic substitutions would likewise not be treated as authentic phosphorylation.\n\nAlternatives, falsification, and limitations. An alternative is that TDP-43 assemblies or stress granules first sequester transport factors, with NUP98 phosphorylation occurring downstream [S3, S4]; another is generalized cellular injury. Compare phosphorylation blockade with independently validated granule-assembly suppression, while monitoring granule-independent assemblies. Standardizing soluble transport factors in permeabilized-cell import assays would help distinguish a retained pore-associated defect from soluble-factor depletion. Control nuclear-envelope integrity, passive permeability, and viability. A falsifying observation would be verified elimination of Ser606 phosphorylation, with normal baseline pore function, that leaves early NUP98 displacement and import impairment unchanged. Phosphorylation appearing only after transport failure would contradict the proposed initiation sequence. Limitations include editing-induced structural effects, kinase pleiotropy, incomplete detection of early assemblies, immature cultured neurons, and uncertain relevance beyond the selected TARDBP context.",
